WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
First, find the fragment.
The camera detects a piece and separates its visible outline from the belt.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.000
Give it a temporary identity.
Software calls this fragment “42” and links its observations as it travels.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Add geometry, if depth is available.
Optional depth sensing could estimate external height and pose.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Predict when 42 reaches each station.
An encoder measures belt travel. Software predicts station arrival.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.000
A changing magnetic field induces currents.
AC in a transmit coil creates a changing field. Closed eddy-current loops form inside a conductor.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Measure the electrical response.
The conductor’s secondary magnetic field changes the receive-coil voltage. Link the measurement to 42.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Try another field direction.
A second station gives another directional view of the same fragment’s response.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Combine observations. Keep uncertainty.
A proposed model combines shape, response and association quality to estimate a routing class.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Send a decision to the existing sorter.
The host receives a proposed route—or recheck—with the piece ID and timing information.

